{% set local_problem %}
<p>Cullman's housing stock tells the story of a community that builds to last. From <a href="/recent-projects/">historic homes near downtown</a> to well-maintained neighborhoods throughout the county, there's an expectation of quality that extends to every aspect of home maintenance.</p>

<p>Traditional sectional gutters don't meet that standard. Made from 10-foot sections joined together, they have seams every 10 feet that eventually leak, catch debris, and look increasingly shabby over time. For homes otherwise meticulously maintained, failing gutters stand out.</p>

<p>We also see a lot of builder-grade systems on newer Cullman homes: .027 gauge aluminum (thin enough to dent when a ladder bumps it), 5-inch troughs that overflow in heavy rain, 2x3 downspouts spaced 50+ feet apart. Technically code-compliant. Practically inadequate for Cullman County's 54+ inches of annual rainfall.</p>

<p><strong>Our opinion:</strong> If your gutters are the weak link on an otherwise well-maintained home, replacement pays off — in appearance, in function, and in preventing the water damage that undermines everything else you're doing to maintain your property.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Seamless gutters are manufactured from continuous aluminum coil, formed on-site to exact lengths required for each run of your home. Instead of 10-foot sections joined together, you get one continuous piece from corner to corner.</p>

<p><strong>What we install:</strong> 6-inch K-style gutters in .032 gauge aluminum. Hidden hangers at 24-inch intervals (tighter than 32-inch code minimum). 3x4 downspouts positioned every 35-40 linear feet. Everything measured and fabricated in your driveway.</p>

<p>For <a href="/recent-projects/">historic homes</a>, we also offer half-round gutters that provide period-appropriate appearance while delivering modern performance. Copper options for high-end restoration where aluminum would look wrong.</p>

<p><strong>The honest bit:</strong> "Seamless" is slightly marketing. You still have seams at inside corners, outside corners, and downspout drops. What you eliminate is the seam every 10 feet in the middle of runs — and those mid-run seams are what fail first.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've installed seamless gutters in Cullman County for over 20 years. We understand that this community expects quality workmanship, and that's what we deliver — proper installation, premium materials, and attention to details that matter.</p>

<p>We're about an hour from Cullman, and we serve the area regularly. When you need us, we're close enough to respond — and we stand behind everything we install.</p>

<p><strong>Warning from experience:</strong> Be cautious of "gutter contractors" offering prices significantly below market. We've replaced a lot of cheap installations in Cullman — thin aluminum that dents and warps, hangers spaced too far apart that allow sagging, inadequate downspouts that cause overflow. Quality work costs what it costs. Cutting corners just means paying twice.</p>
{% endset %}
